In the event of a data leak a hacker might be interested to know if foo@gmail.com is in the database or not. Assuming they did not know the secret this would make it impossible. Also I need something that produces the same hash each time for database lookup purposes.
Does this sound like a good approach? Keen to hear this group's thoughts.

candidate_qwen3_32b:
Your approach has some security issues. Using scrypt for hashing email addresses is not appropriate because scrypt is a key derivation function (KDF), not a hashing algorithm. It is designed for password hashing with built-in salting and work factors, which is unnecessary for your use case.

Instead, use a cryptographic hash function like SHA-256. Also, your salt derivation method (secret + email) is deterministic, which reduces the security benefit of salting. A better approach is to use a fixed secret key with HMAC-SHA256 to derive a unique, deterministic hash:

```javascript
const crypto = require("crypto");
const secret = "secret123";
const email = "foo@gmail.com";
const hashedEmail = crypto
  .createHmac("sha256", secret)
  .update(email)
  .digest("hex");
console.log(hashedEmail);
```

This ensures uniqueness, anonymity, and resistance to preimage attacks if the secret remains confidential.

answer:
According to ISO/IEC 8825-1:2003 BER/DER encoded Integers should be:

8.3.2 If the contents octets of an integer value encoding consist of more than one octet, then the bits of the first octet and bit 8 of the
  second octet: a) shall not all be ones; and b) shall not all be zero.
  NOTE – These rules ensure that an integer value is always encoded in
  the smallest possible number of octets.

apparently dumpasn1 does check exactly this:
    if( i == 0 )
        {
        if( ( value == 0x00 ) && ( ( ch & 0x80 ) == 0x00 ) )
            warnNonDER = TRUE;
        if( ( value == 0xFF ) && ( ( ch & 0x80 ) == 0x80 ) )
            warnNonDER = TRUE;
        if( warnNonDER )
            {
            intBuffer[ 0 ] = ( int ) value;
            intBuffer[ 1 ] = ch;
            }
        }

See: https://github.com/clibs/dumpasn1/blob/master/dumpasn1.c#L1398
So since the encoding says it is BER/DER encoded INTEGER, it should be.
